Revision history for Perl extension Feersum

1.507
        - multi-server instances, multi-listener, new_instance()
        - tls 1.3 (picotls+openssl), per-listener TLS contexts, SNI vhosts
        - http/2 (nghttp2): multiplexing, streaming, extended CONNECT (rfc 8441),
          rapid-reset protection (CVE-2023-44487)
        - proxy protocol v1/v2 with TLV parsing
        - reverse_proxy mode: trust X-Forwarded-For/-Proto from a trusted
          upstream (client_address, url_scheme); off by default
        - ipv6 listeners
        - header/read/write timeouts, max_connections, max_body_len, max_uri_len,
          max_read_buf, max_accept_per_loop
        - active_conns, total_requests, wbuf_low_water,
          max_h2_concurrent_streams, set_psgix_io
        - psgix.h2.trailers, psgix.h2.extended_connect, psgix.h2.protocol,
          psgix.proxy_tlvs
        - ~34% PSGI throughput improvement
        - Runner: hot_restart, preload_app, daemonize, pid_file, user/group,
          after_fork, access_log, graceful_timeout, startup_timeout,
          max_requests_per_worker
        - bin/feersum native mode: full Runner option parity (repeatable
          --listen, --hot-restart, --daemonize, --user/--group, ...)
        - requires Perl 5.14+ (was 5.8.7)
        - Feersum object is no longer a hashref (use public API)
        - psgi.input always valid (PSGI 1.1 compliant)
        - response header/status-line CRLF injection rejected (CWE-113); also
          NUL and other control characters, empty header names, and names
          containing or starting with a space
        - a 1xx as a final response status is rejected (it is interim, so the
          client misattributed every later response on the connection); 101
          stays legal.  A status above 599 now answers 500 on HTTP/1.x, as it
          already did on HTTP/2
        - $r->read($buf, $len, $offset) no longer corrupts the heap when $len
          is negative (it could abort the whole server)
        - $w->write(substr($x, ...)) and send_response(..., substr($x, ...))
          no longer silently write nothing
        - http/2 tunnels (extended CONNECT) now apply the same 16MB
          app-to-client backpressure cap the TLS relay does
        - a tunnelled app that closes its handle with unread inbound data no
          longer has its queued outbound bytes discarded
        - a psgi.input poll_cb that dies now costs that one request (500)
          instead of killing the server process
        - a graceful_shutdown or worker-retirement callback that dies is
          reported instead of silently leaving the server half-shut-down
        - psgi.input close() no longer breaks keepalive or discards an
          already-pipelined next request
        - an http/1.0 keep-alive client that pipelines into one segment now
          gets its second request answered; the buffered request was never
          parsed until unrelated later traffic arrived, or read_timeout
          killed it
        - the header timeout now covers a pipelined request that arrives
          half-written; it had no deadline at all, and stalling one closed
          the connection with no 408
        - graceful shutdown no longer waits out read_timeout on a connection
          that was busy when it began: the idle sweep runs once, and such a
          connection went back to keepalive-idle behind it, so the drain took
          a further 5s by default - the same as Runner's graceful_timeout,
          which then force-exited a worker that had already finished
        - graceful_shutdown releases its hold on the event loop (prepare/check/
          idle watchers and the shared date timer), so shutting down one
          instance no longer keeps the loop alive for the whole process
        - an application that sets Connection: close is now obeyed (rfc 9112
          9.6) instead of having it put on the wire and ignored: the
          connection was parked in keepalive-idle until read_timeout, and the
          server's own header went out alongside the application's, giving
          "close, close" or - to an HTTP/1.0 client asking for keep-alive -
          the contradictory pair "close, keep-alive".  Connection: Upgrade
          still passes through untouched
        - a response to HEAD no longer sends the body (rfc 9110 9.3.2); it
          previously desynchronised a keepalive connection
        - tls: a client offering ALPN with no protocol in common now gets a
          fatal no_application_protocol alert (rfc 7301 3.2) instead of a
          silent fallback to http/1.1
        - 205 Reset Content now sends Content-Length: 0 (rfc 7231 6.3.6); it
          previously had no length delimiter at all on a keepalive connection
        - http/2 stream creation is no longer rejected by max_connections
          (max_h2_concurrent_streams bounds streams); streams still count
          toward active_conns, so they do consume the max_connections budget
        - fix a crash: a psgi handler that dies leaked a perl stack slot per
          request, aborting the process after ~100 such requests (since 2010)
        - psgi env values are per-request copies: assigning REMOTE_ADDR,
          REQUEST_METHOD or psgi.errors no longer leaks into a later request
          on the same connection, nor croaks on a read-only value
        - fix a remote dos: one http/2 HEADERS+RST_STREAM pair killed the
          worker process. the reset freed the stream before the app answered,
          and the response path then croaked where no eval could catch it -
          a single ~50-byte write, so the rapid-reset counter never engaged
        - every response now carries a Date header (rfc 9110 6.6.1); it was
          emitted only on HTTP/1.1, so HTTP/1.0 clients and every error
          response got none
        - fix a memory leak: every 4xx/5xx the request reader generates itself
          (malformed request, too many header fields, oversize header block,
          long uri, oversize body) leaked its entire response buffer, ~256
          bytes each, so a stream of bad requests grew the worker without
          bound. the response sv was mortalised inside an ev watcher callback,
          where nothing ever runs FREETMPS to reap it
        - fix cross-connection damage after a psgix.io/io() takeover: the app's
          IO handle SHARES the socket (new_from_fd opens "+<&=", it does not
          dup), so once the app closed it the kernel could hand that descriptor
          to the next accept, and the connection's later DESTROY closed it a
          second time - shutting down an unrelated live connection, silently,
          with both syscalls succeeding. feersum now declines to close a
          descriptor it gave away. this also removes the "close ... Bad file
          descriptor" noise an app saw when it closed its own psgix.io handle
        - a 405 for an unsupported http/1.x method now carries the Allow header
          rfc 9110 15.5.6 requires. note the restriction itself is documented
          now too: http/1.x accepts only GET/HEAD/POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE/OPTIONS
          and answers anything else, CONNECT included, with 405; http/2 passes
          every method through. http/1.x tunnels go through psgix.io, not the
          CONNECT method, which only http/2 has (rfc 8441 extended connect)
        - max_connections and max_connection_reqs no longer read back as 0,
          meaning unlimited, when set to a value too large for the internal
          counter: a cap of 2**31 or more silently became no cap at all
        - Runner: a fork() failure while respawning a worker no longer strands
          the slot for good. the croak ran inside an ev watcher callback, where
          ev catches and discards it, so nothing armed a retry and the worker
          never came back; without SO_REUSEPORT the supervisor also re-listens
          across the respawn fork and the skipped unlisten left it serving
          requests itself, which with pre_fork=1 is a silent degradation to a
          single process that was never configured to serve. the fork is now
          eval'd, the unlisten unconditional, and a failure retries through the
          existing respawn backoff
        - nine psgi env booleans (psgi.streaming, psgi.nonblocking,
          psgi.run_once, psgi.multithread, psgi.multiprocess and the four
          psgix.* flags) were the shared immortals PL_sv_yes/no rather than
          per-request copies, so middleware assigning to one died with
          "Modification of a read-only value attempted" and the request became
          a 500. Plack::Middleware::BufferedStreaming escaped only because
          Plack::Util::TRUE is itself PL_sv_yes, which sv_setsv short-circuits
        - perf: the per-request request struct no longer zeroes its 2 KB header
          array. only [0, num_headers) is ever read, and that memset was ~20%
          of the native per-request instruction count
        - perf: tls output is encrypted straight into the connection write
          buffer instead of into a scratch buffer that was then copied. saves a
          full copy of every ciphertext byte plus a malloc/free per record
          batch: ~42k fewer instructions per 16 KB TLS response
        - a zero-length write truncated the response it was part of. alone in
          a write batch it makes write()/writev() return 0, which the plain
          transport read as a fatal error and shut the response down mid-body;
          under keepalive the connection was then reused, so a client honouring
          Content-Length read the next response's status line as the tail of
          this body. reachable from an empty $w->write() or a PSGI IO-handle
          body whose getline returns an empty string between chunks. the TLS
          path already skipped zero-length iovecs, so the two disagreed
        - an app dying inside a streaming callback produced a response no
          client could tell from a complete one: the die unwound the writer
          before the error was caught, and the writer's destructor is the
          ordinary clean-completion path, so http/1 still sent the terminating
          chunk and http/2 set END_STREAM on a truncated body. the writer is
          now held across the callback and a mid-stream die seals the response
          dirty instead: http/1 closes without the terminator, http/2 sends
          RST_STREAM. a response close()d before the die, a writer the app
          still holds, and $@ left over from a caught inner eval are all
          unaffected
        - a write poll_cb that declined an invitation (returned without
          writing) was re-invited by the level-triggered write watcher every
          loop iteration: ~1M calls/sec, a full core, on plain and TLS; the
          http/2 pump had the opposite defect and stalled such a stream until
          the peer happened to send a frame. all transports now agree: a
          decline parks the response and re-invites on a paced backoff (1ms
          doubling to 100ms, reset by progress), a write from any other event
          still resumes immediately, and an empty write() still requests an
          immediate re-invitation. a parked response has no write deadline,
          matching the long-poll rule, and a vanished client is noticed at
          the next paced retry (on TLS only when the close was abrupt; a
          graceful close_notify still waits for the next write, as documented
          for quiet streams)
        - see the UPGRADING section in the Feersum docs for behaviour changes
          since 1.505 that need no code change to bite

1.506 Thu Jan 16 00:00:00 2026 +0300
        Performance:
        - free-lists for iomatrix, feer_req and rinq structs
        - cache remote_addr/remote_port per connection
        - embed sockaddr_storage in feer_conn struct
        - cached SVs for common HTTP status codes
        - ASCII case conversion lookup tables
        - improved likely/unlikely branch hints

        Benchmarks (vs 1.505, keepalive mode, h2load -c100 -t2 -D10s):
        - TCP PSGI: 139K req/s (+42% vs 98K in 1.505)
        - TCP Native: 219K req/s (+61% vs 135K in 1.505)
        - Unix socket PSGI: 184K req/s (+32% vs TCP)
        - Unix socket Native: 352K req/s (+61% vs TCP)

        Features:
        - Expect: 100-continue support for HTTP/1.1 clients
        - Transfer-Encoding: chunked request body support
        - Native interface io() method for protocol upgrades (WebSocket, etc.)
        - Native interface return_from_io() for keepalive after io() access
        - PSGI return_from_psgix_io() for keepalive after psgix.io access
        - poll_cb support on Reader handles for streaming input
        - pause_accept/resume_accept methods for backpressure control
        - sendfile() zero-copy file transfer (Linux)
        - SO_REUSEPORT for better prefork scaling
        - EPOLLEXCLUSIVE for prefork thundering herd mitigation (no libev patching needed)
        - configurable libev watcher priorities (read_priority, write_priority, accept_priority)

        Security:
        - Request smuggling prevention: reject requests with both
          Content-Length and Transfer-Encoding headers (RFC 7230)
        - Reject obsolete header line folding (obs-fold) per RFC 7230
        - DoS protection: MAX_TRAILER_HEADERS limit for chunked requests

        Bugfixes:
        - fix uninitialized pointer in feersum_env_headers (potential UB)
        - fix off-by-one in uri_decode_sv bounds check
        - fix fcntl calls to preserve existing file descriptor flags
        - fix RESPOND_SHUTDOWN debug string value
        - fix av_extend argument for psgi.version array
        - fix max_connection_reqs type handling for negative values
        - fix test files missing 'use blib' (t/17-priority.t, t/56-leak-extended.t)
        - fix error message consistency (remove trailing periods)
        - fix chunked encoding for streaming responses with Content-Length
        - fix io_taken flag reset in return_from_psgix_io for keepalive
        - fix missing HEADER_NORM_UPCASE_DASH in @EXPORT_OK

        Code quality:
        - add thread-safety documentation and comments to non-thread-safe structures
        - add unlikely() to error paths consistently
        - add trailer_count field to connection struct for chunked parsing state
        - improve assert messages with descriptions
        - remove dead code: unused str_eq function, obs-fold handlers,
          RINQ_IS_UNINIT and RINQ_IS_ATTACHED macros
        - remove unused forward declaration feersum_set_keepalive
        - remove unused crlf_sv variable
        - remove unused char* n in feersum_env_headers
        - remove redundant conditional in keepalive reset
        - add missing static keyword to ev_idle ei declaration
        - add default case to switch in feersum_env_headers
        - use STRLEN instead of int for buffer sizes
        - improve multiline header tracking variable naming
        - fix typos: theif -> thief, probaby -> probably
        - added OOM check in rinq allocation with croak()
        - update outdated documentation for poll_cb on Reader handles
        - update outdated documentation for keepalive support
        - refactor: extract send_100_continue() helper to deduplicate code
        - refactor: extract free_request() helper to deduplicate request cleanup
        - refactor: extract feersum_return_from_io() shared helper for io return

